    "seo": {
      "title": "Sadeh: Fire and the Winter Calendar | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Sadeh’s communal fires, winter timing, agricultural associations, and the distinction between historical evidence and origin legends."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "season-and-fire",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A winter turning point",
        "content": "Sadeh, also written Sada, is marked by the kindling of fires. Encyclopaedia Iranica places its familiar calendar date on 10 Bahman, around 30 January: after the winter solstice rather than on it. UNESCO’s contemporary heritage description gives 30 January as the annual date and presents the celebration as a turning point toward preparation for spring cultivation. [s1][s2]\n\nThe name is often connected with the Persian word for one hundred. UNESCO explains this through the days and nights remaining before spring. The scholarly literature records competing explanations and treats the etymology as uncertain. A customary explanation can carry cultural meaning without settling the word’s historical origin. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "history-and-legend",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "History alongside origin stories",
        "content": "Historical and literary sources document Sadeh festivities at Muslim courts between the tenth and twelfth centuries. The festival’s history therefore extends beyond a simple division between pre-Islamic celebration and later disappearance. [s2]\n\nOrigin traditions attach Sadeh to several figures. One associates it with Hushang, a legendary king whose attempt to strike a dragon with a stone reveals fire. This is an explanatory legend, not a securely dated account of the festival’s foundation. Iranica also documents Iranian Zoroastrian observance, while showing that historical dates and interpretations have not been uniform. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "community-and-land",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "More than a bonfire",
        "content": "UNESCO’s shared Iran–Tajikistan record describes singing, dancing, prayer, and offerings of fruit around a fire. Agricultural associations include pruning and preparing land. It also records collective work after the celebration, such as clearing waterways and repairing bridges. These activities place seasonal renewal within the practical maintenance of community life. [s1]\n\nPractices vary between the two countries and among communities; the nomination’s inventory should not be read as the program of every Iranian gathering. Knowledge passes through observation, participation, storytelling, and the preparation of food, with publications and media providing additional channels. [s1]\n\nSadeh entered UNESCO’s Representative List in 2023. That recognition identifies a living heritage practice; it supplies neither a count of participants nor proof of unchanged observance across centuries. The clearest introduction to Sadeh holds its fire, seasonal setting, and diverse historical interpretations together. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
