    "description": "Mehregan is an Iranian seasonal festival with historical associations with Mehr, or Mithra, and contemporary forms centered on harvest thanksgiving. Its dates, religious expressions, and festivities vary, including the practices jointly recognized for Iran and Tajikistan by UNESCO. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Mehregan", "Autumn traditions", "Harvest celebrations", "Mithra", "Intangible heritage"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:54:15Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:54:15Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Mehregan: Harvest, Mehr, and Living Tradition | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Mehregan’s historical associations, calendar differences, harvest tables, and varied religious and community celebrations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "mehr-and-autumn",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Mehr and the autumn festival",
        "content": "Mehregan takes its name from Mehr, the Iranian deity also known as Mithra. Scholarly discussion connects Mithra with covenants and arbitration, but the festival’s earliest development remains debated. An association with an ancient deity does not by itself establish one definitive founding event or an unchanged ceremony. [s2]\n\nIn the traditional religious calendar, the feast falls when the day named Mehr meets the month named Mehr. Iranica identifies this as the sixteenth day of the seventh month, while noting that some almanacs mark 10 Mehr following reform of the Iranian calendar in 1925. These differing calendar references should not be flattened into a universal Gregorian anniversary. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-interpretations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A history with several interpretations",
        "content": "Historical accounts connect Mehregan with royal celebration, gifts, and renewal. Epic traditions also associate it with the rebellion of Kaveh and the triumph of Fereydun over Zahhak. These legendary figures explain the festival through justice and the defeat of tyranny; their stories are not independently dated records of its origin. [s2]\n\nThe scholarly literature describes changes in the festival’s duration and placement as calendars evolved. It also discusses competing interpretations of its relationship with Mithra and the equinox. This complexity matters: a neat claim that modern festivities reproduce a single ancient ceremony would exceed the evidence. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "harvest-and-community",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Thanksgiving in different communities",
        "content": "UNESCO’s contemporary record describes Mehregan in Iran and Tajikistan as a religious celebration connected with livestock and crop harvests. It gives a celebration window from 2 October to 2 November across the nominated practices, rather than one date binding every community. [s1]\n\nShared features include tables displaying produce and grain, prepared dishes, nuts, sweets, music, and dancing. Religious expression differs: the record describes Zoroastrian scriptural recitation and Muslim prayers. It also includes exhibitions of flowers and handicrafts, theatrical performances, and local sports. These are examples across the documented tradition, not requirements for every gathering. [s1]\n\nThe jointly nominated ceremony entered UNESCO’s Representative List in 2024. Its account emphasizes learning through stories and participation, alongside educational institutions. Reading Mehregan through these activities brings attention to the people who prepare, perform, and transmit it. The harvest setting and varied observances make local context essential to understanding any particular celebration. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
